1
00:00:01,718 --> 00:00:04,059
[SPEAKER_01]: In the Buddhist writings, you often hear about delusion.

2
00:00:04,580 --> 00:00:05,800
[SPEAKER_01]: Greed, aversion, delusion.

3
00:00:08,081 --> 00:00:09,162
[SPEAKER_00]: All about dosa and moha.

4
00:00:11,263 --> 00:00:27,852
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know, people can get their head around greed, can get their head around aversion, but often delusion is kind of regarded as as something that's not directly my responsibility, inasmuch as greed is, or aversion.

5
00:00:28,352 --> 00:00:29,353
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know what I've done now.

6
00:00:30,152 --> 00:00:30,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

7
00:00:30,612 --> 00:00:40,816
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's regarded in sort of a semi-metaphysical sense, as in, it's there, but it's not really something I can directly experience.

8
00:00:41,676 --> 00:00:42,496
[SPEAKER_01]: But actually, it is.

9
00:00:43,217 --> 00:00:52,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Delusion is an attitude of your mind in as much greed or lust and aversion are.

10
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:56,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, it wouldn't have been mentioned always like that.

11
00:00:59,947 --> 00:01:10,850
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from that point of view, delusion, it's more of like an attitude of indolence, laziness...

12
00:01:13,531 --> 00:01:14,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Choice to keep things cloudy.

13
00:01:16,011 --> 00:01:16,351
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

14
00:01:16,671 --> 00:01:25,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Keeping it opaque intentionally, not look... turning a blind eye, distracting yourself intentionally with things that are going to take your mind away from yourself.

15
00:01:27,174 --> 00:01:29,715
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize delusion is a very active thing.

16
00:01:30,888 --> 00:01:33,508
[SPEAKER_01]: And, as the Buddha said, that's why it's the most blameworthy, actually.

17
00:01:33,629 --> 00:01:35,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's the hardest to undo as well.

18
00:01:37,049 --> 00:01:41,510
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact that you can undo it, means it's not this metaphysical lack of knowledge.

19
00:01:42,610 --> 00:01:43,610
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your very attitude.

20
00:01:45,471 --> 00:01:48,911
[SPEAKER_01]: That it's even more fundamental than the attitude of lust and the attitude of aversion.

21
00:01:49,271 --> 00:01:50,451
[SPEAKER_00]: They're kind of secondary to it.

22
00:01:53,272 --> 00:01:55,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Lust requires a basis of delusion.

23
00:01:56,793 --> 00:01:58,893
[SPEAKER_01]: Aversion requires a basis of delusion.

24
00:02:00,083 --> 00:02:01,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Delusion requires a base of delusion.

25
00:02:04,487 --> 00:02:16,158
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're deluded through and through, not because you don't know certain things, but because you perpetually act out of ignorance, that then maintains yourself in the situation of ignorance.

26
00:02:16,178 --> 00:02:19,981
[SPEAKER_01]: And situation of ignorance, again, it's not ignorance-ignorance, as in you don't know something.

27
00:02:20,442 --> 00:02:23,885
[SPEAKER_01]: It's you actively ignoring and giving priority to the ignoring of it.

28
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:32,661
[SPEAKER_01]: So, choosing is... choosing to distract yourself, for example.

29
00:02:33,942 --> 00:02:49,687
[SPEAKER_01]: So, actively... actively making a choice, that would result in certain actions, that are not necessarily lustful or hateful, but are based upon, you know, wanting to be self-aware, for example.

30
00:02:51,568 --> 00:02:56,590
[SPEAKER_01]: So, entertainment, or distraction in general.

31
00:02:56,670 --> 00:02:57,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'm bored by myself,

32
00:02:58,433 --> 00:03:00,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I'm aware of everything and it's very unpleasant.

33
00:03:01,234 --> 00:03:03,194
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I just go and chat to some people, randomly.

34
00:03:03,275 --> 00:03:08,836
[SPEAKER_01]: Not for sensual reasons or because I hate them, just because I want something to do.

35
00:03:08,856 --> 00:03:23,461
[SPEAKER_01]: So, often needing things to do is rooted in delusion, as in in distraction, as in indolence, on the level of... Like, you can be very diligent externally, he's doing all this work, but the reasons for you doing that work are rooted in you

36
00:03:24,820 --> 00:03:27,341
[SPEAKER_01]: wanting to turn a blind eye in regard to yourself.

37
00:03:27,861 --> 00:03:29,341
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to be self-aware, basically.

38
00:03:29,361 --> 00:03:31,462
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't want to see the truth of your mind.

39
00:03:32,222 --> 00:03:37,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, because that will result in the unpleasant truths of your mind, or simply unpleasant states of boredom and so on.

40
00:03:38,664 --> 00:03:39,864
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I better find something to do.

41
00:03:39,904 --> 00:03:41,405
[SPEAKER_01]: I better find an external purpose.

42
00:03:41,765 --> 00:03:43,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, that doing...

43
00:03:43,465 --> 00:03:50,207
[SPEAKER_01]: It will occupy my mind, so my mind won't be aware of itself, because being aware of itself is too unpleasant, is too threatening.

44
00:03:51,255 --> 00:03:53,036
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a way of keep covering up the truth.

45
00:03:53,897 --> 00:03:54,317
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

46
00:03:55,078 --> 00:03:59,601
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the way of maintaining yourself in a state of cover-up.

47
00:04:00,902 --> 00:04:01,943
[SPEAKER_00]: Keep the dust moving.

48
00:04:02,163 --> 00:04:02,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

49
00:04:03,043 --> 00:04:06,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, if it settles, you get to see what's there and it's unpleasant.

50
00:04:06,686 --> 00:04:07,607
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing.

51
00:04:07,667 --> 00:04:08,727
[SPEAKER_00]: You say self-aware.

52
00:04:09,288 --> 00:04:10,068
[SPEAKER_00]: It sounds like a good thing.

53
00:04:10,669 --> 00:04:13,250
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's like the most painful thing.

54
00:04:13,351 --> 00:04:14,631
[SPEAKER_00]: It is.

55
00:04:14,731 --> 00:04:15,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

56
00:04:15,752 --> 00:04:16,593
[SPEAKER_00]: Most people avoid.

57
00:04:16,913 --> 00:04:19,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's why solitude is very unpleasant to begin with.

58
00:04:20,938 --> 00:04:27,420
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the... as the Buddha himself said many times, he practices so you develop the pleasure of solitude.

59
00:04:27,620 --> 00:04:28,680
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't get it right away.

60
00:04:28,860 --> 00:04:30,161
[SPEAKER_01]: If done rightly, it's unpleasant.

61
00:04:31,841 --> 00:04:37,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're going against the... against the grain of all the distraction you got used to it and that your existence depends upon.

62
00:04:39,344 --> 00:04:39,804
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's it.

63
00:04:40,104 --> 00:04:49,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when you get into like... when you stop maintaining all these attitudes of... they were based upon

64
00:04:52,085 --> 00:04:53,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Obscuring the self-awareness.

65
00:04:54,566 --> 00:04:57,867
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you stop feeding those attitudes, the self-awareness increases.

66
00:04:58,987 --> 00:05:05,369
[SPEAKER_01]: But it feels like the, you know, the boredom starts giving rise to dread and anxiety.

67
00:05:05,409 --> 00:05:07,569
[SPEAKER_01]: Feels like you yourself are drowning.

68
00:05:07,910 --> 00:05:08,590
[SPEAKER_01]: It's crushing you.

69
00:05:09,670 --> 00:05:15,032
[SPEAKER_01]: But what is actually crushing is your assumed level of your own being, the one that you've been proliferating.

70
00:05:18,513 --> 00:05:19,913
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not actually crushing you.

71
00:05:21,317 --> 00:05:27,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Like being alone in your room, not seeing or talking to anyone, cannot do anything to you.

72
00:05:28,878 --> 00:05:31,379
[SPEAKER_01]: But yeah, your mind can sort of drown in it.

73
00:05:32,499 --> 00:05:38,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the mind became dependent upon a certain degree of engagement with the world.

74
00:05:38,200 --> 00:05:39,300
[SPEAKER_01]: That is its threshold.

75
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:42,261
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not a static thing, that threshold.

76
00:05:42,901 --> 00:05:45,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means the more you engage, the more dependent upon engagement you become.

77
00:05:47,262 --> 00:05:49,082
[SPEAKER_01]: The less you engage, well,

78
00:05:49,713 --> 00:05:58,418
[SPEAKER_01]: Initially, it might hurt the lesser engagement, because you used to engage more, but then you get used to that, and then it will not be unpleasant.

79
00:05:59,498 --> 00:06:16,507
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, like you have a very hectic person, who's been sort of doing a lot, engaging a lot, working a lot, for whatever reasons, and then, if he was made to sit down and stop engaging to that level, the discrepancy would be just too much to handle.

80
00:06:17,747 --> 00:06:27,315
[SPEAKER_01]: But if he gradually starts doing that, and then starts ceasing his engagement, he'll get used to that new level of lacking the engagement that he used to have.

81
00:06:28,216 --> 00:06:31,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you are responsible for the threshold of where your being is.

82
00:06:33,881 --> 00:06:37,244
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when he says his consciousness gets established upon that.

83
00:06:38,765 --> 00:06:39,926
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, that becomes the norm.

84
00:06:39,946 --> 00:06:41,347
[SPEAKER_01]: That becomes what you're used to.

85
00:06:41,407 --> 00:06:44,570
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means anything less than that is going to fill you with anxiety and dread.

86
00:06:50,080 --> 00:06:52,441
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's important to recognize that it's not a static thing.

87
00:06:54,702 --> 00:07:04,947
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through using engagement with the world, actions, meaningless work, whatever else, or not even finding meaning for your work, whatever.

88
00:07:05,507 --> 00:07:12,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Practically, you're engaging with the world for the means of escaping self-awareness.

89
00:07:16,352 --> 00:07:18,273
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you will become dependent

90
00:07:19,611 --> 00:07:25,576
[SPEAKER_01]: On that level of engagement, anything less than that engagement will bring you back to your self-awareness.

91
00:07:28,198 --> 00:07:37,766
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that means then you have to keep maintaining the level of your engagement and, once you get used to it, you have to keep increasing it, if you try to use it to avoid self-awareness.

92
00:07:38,507 --> 00:07:40,148
[SPEAKER_01]: And most people, to a degree, are using that.

93
00:07:40,168 --> 00:07:41,910
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why most people are afraid to be alone.

94
00:07:42,770 --> 00:07:54,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Not afraid from like some, you know, monsters and stuff, but afraid just in broad daylight being left alone in the room for a whole day, without doing anything, without taking my mind away from myself.

95
00:07:55,075 --> 00:07:58,537
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why most people would be afraid to be left alone.

96
00:07:58,617 --> 00:08:02,618
[SPEAKER_01]: It would result in experiencing anxiety and existential dread.

97
00:08:04,879 --> 00:08:08,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the human life on average is about avoiding that self-awareness.

98
00:08:09,443 --> 00:08:12,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Which ties in with avoiding irresponsibility and feeds one another and so on.

99
00:08:14,345 --> 00:08:18,127
[SPEAKER_00]: But self-awareness is the thing that's going to make things right.

100
00:08:18,247 --> 00:08:19,048
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

101
00:08:19,448 --> 00:08:25,291
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why people are, because of their own actions, they're liable to suffering.

102
00:08:25,471 --> 00:08:25,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of that.

103
00:08:26,071 --> 00:08:29,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because of some someone else's fault or some design.

104
00:08:31,074 --> 00:08:37,957
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because you act in a manner that keeps making you liable to suffer, keeps you exposed.

105
00:08:41,407 --> 00:08:42,247
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could then...

106
00:08:44,288 --> 00:08:48,988
[SPEAKER_01]: If you take responsibility for it, that's where what I'm saying might become apparent.

107
00:08:50,529 --> 00:08:57,130
[SPEAKER_01]: That the level of engagement, the level of distraction, the level of turning a blind eye, it's not a static thing.

108
00:08:57,570 --> 00:08:58,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you can...

109
00:08:58,570 --> 00:09:03,151
[SPEAKER_01]: The way you've been kind of proliferating it, you can then also undo it, if you stop engaging with it.

110
00:09:05,332 --> 00:09:07,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can certainly put a brakes on its growth.

111
00:09:11,020 --> 00:09:12,761
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the bhava in the sutta is being.

112
00:09:12,961 --> 00:09:14,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, it's not a metaphysical thing.

113
00:09:15,002 --> 00:09:19,125
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the level of your dependence on the senses, which is central being.

114
00:09:20,826 --> 00:09:29,431
[SPEAKER_01]: The level of dependence on the sense of the world, the duty, the perception thing you perceive around, the level you depend on it.

115
00:09:30,932 --> 00:09:32,833
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you can start depending less.

116
00:09:34,254 --> 00:09:37,196
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Arahant has cessation of his being.

117
00:09:37,836 --> 00:09:39,077
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't depend on anything.

118
00:09:40,554 --> 00:09:41,434
[SPEAKER_01]: But he's still there.

119
00:09:42,475 --> 00:09:45,215
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that the being means destruction.

120
00:09:45,235 --> 00:09:45,376
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

121
00:09:45,416 --> 00:09:48,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Being means assumption.

122
00:09:48,937 --> 00:09:50,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Assumption of engagement.

123
00:09:50,097 --> 00:09:52,238
[SPEAKER_01]: Assumption of avoiding responsibility.

124
00:09:52,278 --> 00:09:53,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Assumption of sensuality.

125
00:09:56,359 --> 00:09:57,619
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what delusion is.

126
00:09:59,440 --> 00:10:04,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignoring, turning a blind eye, distracting yourself, not being able to live with yourself.

127
00:10:06,002 --> 00:10:09,243
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, you can ask, back onto that practical level that we always do,

128
00:10:09,943 --> 00:10:13,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it that I don't want to be self-aware?

129
00:10:14,266 --> 00:10:16,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it that I need to distract myself from myself?

130
00:10:18,129 --> 00:10:19,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's unpleasant.

131
00:10:21,331 --> 00:10:26,695
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why am I then maintaining this whole level of being for which I'm responsible?

132
00:10:26,835 --> 00:10:29,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Level of how much I depend on engagement with things.

133
00:10:30,157 --> 00:10:31,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's because I don't want to suffer.

134
00:10:34,669 --> 00:10:37,431
[SPEAKER_00]: But failing to see that my very engagement is...

135
00:10:37,511 --> 00:10:39,672
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the root of your suffering, exactly, yeah.

136
00:10:41,013 --> 00:10:42,194
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why do I suffer then?

137
00:10:42,734 --> 00:10:44,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's because I keep running away from suffering.

138
00:10:44,735 --> 00:10:45,496
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I suffer.

139
00:10:47,657 --> 00:10:50,999
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I stop running away from suffering, I at least have a chance to not suffer.

